### Trino version

481-SNAPSHOT (master)

### Please describe the bug

Hi ! 

I was investigating the es connector performance characteristics and running benchmarks, and I stumbled on this issue. It seems that expansive queries keep running on elasticsearch after they are cancelled on the trino side.  When Trino cancels a query (timeout or user cancellation), the HTTP connection is never closed, so ES gets no signal to stop.

### Repro

```
./mvnw test -pl plugin/trino-elasticsearch -Dtest=TestElasticsearchQueryTimeout -Dair.check.skip-all=true
```

The test runs an intentionally slow `script_score` query with `query_max_execution_time = 100ms`, waits 5 seconds after Trino returns the timeout error, then asserts that `GET _tasks?actions=*search*` has no non-cancelled search tasks. It currently fails: the tasks are still running with `cancelled: false`.

<details>
<summary>
<code>TestElasticsearchQueryTimeout.java</code>
</summary>

```java
package io.trino.plugin.elasticsearch;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.json.JsonMapper;
import io.airlift.json.JsonMapperProvider;
import io.trino.testing.AbstractTestQueryFramework;
import io.trino.testing.QueryRunner;
import org.elasticsearch.client.Request;
import org.elasticsearch.client.RestHighLevelClient;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.List;

import static io.trino.plugin.elasticsearch.ElasticsearchServer.ELASTICSEARCH_8_IMAGE;
import static io.trino.testing.TestingSession.testSessionBuilder;
import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

final class TestElasticsearchQueryTimeout
        extends AbstractTestQueryFramework
{
    private static final JsonMapper JSON = new JsonMapperProvider().get();

    private RestHighLevelClient esClient;

    @Override
    protected QueryRunner createQueryRunner()
            throws Exception
    {
        ElasticsearchServer elasticsearch = closeAfterClass(new ElasticsearchServer(ELASTICSEARCH_8_IMAGE));
        esClient = closeAfterClass(elasticsearch.getClient());
        return ElasticsearchQueryRunner.builder(elasticsearch).build();
    }

    @Test
    void testCancelledQueryStopsRunningOnEs()
            throws Exception
    {
        Request put = new Request("PUT", "/test/_doc/1?refresh");
        put.setJsonEntity("{\"v\":1}");
        esClient.getLowLevelClient().performRequest(put);

        try {
            // slow query: O(n2) string concat with painless scripting
            String esQuery = """
                    {
                        "query": {
                            "script_score": {
                                "query": {"match_all": {}},
                                "script": {
                                    "source": "String s = new String(); for (int i = 0; i < 999999; i++) { s = s + Integer.toString(i % 10); } return s.length();"
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }""".replace("\n", " ");
            try {
                getQueryRunner().execute(
                        testSessionBuilder()
                                .setCatalog("elasticsearch").setSchema("tpch")
                                .setSystemProperty("query_max_execution_time", "100ms")
                                .build(),
                        "SELECT result FROM TABLE(elasticsearch.system.raw_query(" +
                                "schema => 'tpch', " +
                                "index => 'test', " +
                                "query => '" + esQuery + "'" +
                                "))");
            }
            catch (Exception e) {
                System.out.println("Trino query failed as expected: " + e.getMessage());
            }

            Thread.sleep(5000);

            List<String> tasks = getSearchTasks();
            System.out.println("ES search tasks 5s after Trino timeout: " + tasks);

            assertThat(tasks)
                    .as("ES should have no active search tasks 5s after Trino cancelled the query")
                    .isEmpty();
        }
        finally {
            esClient.getLowLevelClient().performRequest(new Request("DELETE", "/test"));
        }
    }

    private List<String> getSearchTasks()
            throws IOException
    {
        Request request = new Request("GET", "/_tasks");
        request.addParameter("actions", "*search*");
        request.addParameter("detailed", "true");
        JsonNode nodes = JSON.readTree(
                esClient.getLowLevelClient().performRequest(request)
                        .getEntity().getContent()).get("nodes");
        List<String> descriptions = new ArrayList<>();
        if (nodes != null) {
            for (Iterator<JsonNode> it = nodes.elements(); it.hasNext(); ) {
                JsonNode tasks = it.next().get("tasks");
                if (tasks != null) {
                    for (Iterator<JsonNode> taskIt = tasks.elements(); taskIt.hasNext(); ) {
                        JsonNode task = taskIt.next();
                        if (!task.path("cancelled").asBoolean(false)) {
                            descriptions.add(task.get("description").asText());
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return descriptions;
    }
}
```

</details>


#### outside trino

This is what I ran to confirm the issue was in trino: running the same es query with curl with a short timeout, I can see that the corresponding tasks get cancelled on the ES side:

```bash
docker run -d --name es -p 9200:9200 -e discovery.type=single-node -e xpack.security.enabled=false elasticsearch:8.11.3
curl -s -X PUT localhost:9200/test/_doc/1?refresh -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"v":1}'
# send the slow query, close connection after 100ms
curl --max-time 0.1 -X GET localhost:9200/test/_search -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"query":{"script_score":{"query":{"match_all":{}},"script":{"source":"String s = new String(); for (int i = 0; i < 999999; i++) { s = s + Integer.toString(i % 10); } return s.length();"}}}}'
# check tasks: cancelled: true
sleep 2 && curl -s 'localhost:9200/_tasks?actions=*search*' | jq
```

The tasks show `"cancelled": true`.

### probable root cause

(ai-generated, included in case this helps. I haven't checked this yet)
**edit** I checked it now, it's true.

All ES HTTP calls use `RestClient.performRequest()`, which blocks on `Future.get()`.

When Trino cancels:

1. `QueryTracker` (1s poll) detects the timeout and calls `Thread.interrupt()` on the worker thread
2. `Future.get()` throws `InterruptedException`. The future is still pending, but noone is waiting for its results.
3. The HTTP connection was opened on a different thread, and stays open. ES never receives a disconnect. The query runs to completion.

The only upper bound is `elasticsearch.request-timeout` (default 10s). If set high to accommodate legitimate slow queries, cancelled queries waste ES resources for the full duration.

