### Trino version

479

### Please describe the bug

Relevant settings:
* Spooling is enabled and configured to spool to an Azure storage account
* Reproduced with Trino JDBC driver version 479 and 481

### Description
We observed that a thread executing a Trino query was indefinitely waiting on `semaphore.acquire()` in the `AsyncResultIterator`:
```
"reports-1" #49 [122] prio=5 os_prio=0 cpu=933035.09ms elapsed=1130854.57s tid=0x0000776b0410cf00 nid=122 waiting on condition  [0x0000776b024e9000]
   java.lang.Thread.State: WAITING (parking)
        at jdk.internal.misc.Unsafe.park(java.base@21.0.5/Native Method)
        - parking to wait for  <0x0000000712606680> (a java.util.concurrent.Semaphore$NonfairSync)
        at java.util.concurrent.locks.LockSupport.park(java.base@21.0.5/LockSupport.java:221)
        at java.util.concurrent.locks.AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.acquire(java.base@21.0.5/AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.java:754)
        at java.util.concurrent.locks.AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.acquireSharedInterruptibly(java.base@21.0.5/AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.java:1099)
        at java.util.concurrent.Semaphore.acquire(java.base@21.0.5/Semaphore.java:318)
        at io.trino.jdbc.AsyncResultIterator.computeNext(AsyncResultIterator.java:146)
        at io.trino.jdbc.AsyncResultIterator.computeNext(AsyncResultIterator.java:41)
        at io.trino.jdbc.$internal.guava.collect.AbstractIterator.tryToComputeNext(AbstractIterator.java:141)
        at io.trino.jdbc.$internal.guava.collect.AbstractIterator.hasNext(AbstractIterator.java:136)
        at io.trino.jdbc.$internal.client.CloseableLimitingIterator.computeNext(CloseableLimitingIterator.java:55)
        at io.trino.jdbc.$internal.guava.collect.AbstractIterator.tryToComputeNext(AbstractIterator.java:141)
        at io.trino.jdbc.$internal.guava.collect.AbstractIterator.hasNext(AbstractIterator.java:136)
        at io.trino.jdbc.AbstractTrinoResultSet.next(AbstractTrinoResultSet.java:217)
        at io.trino.jdbc.TrinoResultSet.next(TrinoResultSet.java:33)
        at org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate$RowCallbackHandlerResultSetExtractor.extractData(JdbcTemplate.java:1769)
        at org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate$1.doInPreparedStatement(JdbcTemplate.java:733)
        at org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate.execute(JdbcTemplate.java:658)
        at org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate.query(JdbcTemplate.java:723)
```

In `AsyncResultIterator`, permits are released for the semaphore when a full batch of rows is processed inside the loop, or outside the loop to handle the final partial batch of rows.

If an exception is thrown while calling `client.advance()` in the loop condition, and a partial batch of rows has been processed, permits for the partial batch are never released.
```java
try {
    int rowsProcessed = 0;
    do {
        QueryStatusInfo results = client.currentStatusInfo();
        progressCallback.accept(QueryStats.create(results.getId(), results.getStats()));
        warningsManager.addWarnings(results.getWarnings());
        for (List<Object> row : client.currentRows()) {
            rowQueue.put(row);
            if (++rowsProcessed == BATCH_SIZE) {
                semaphore.release(rowsProcessed);
                rowsProcessed = 0;
            }
        }
    }
    while (!cancelled && client.advance()); // Exception thrown
    if (rowsProcessed > 0) {
        semaphore.release(rowsProcessed); // Skipped when exception thrown above
    }

    ...
}
catch (CancellationException | InterruptedException e) {
    ...
}
finally {
    finished = true; // Skips to here
    semaphore.release();
}
```

A call to `computeNext()` will block indefinitely on `semaphore.acquire()`, because the row queue is not empty, but not enough permits have been released. No exception is thrown or logged, since `future.get()` is only called after acquiring a permit.

Expected behavior:
* Calls to `computeNext()` do not block indefinitely if such an exception occurs
* The exception is propagated to the caller

A possible fix and test that reproduces the issue can be found here: https://github.com/trinodb/trino/pull/30008

### Additional Background & Possible Reproduction Steps

Here is a pseudocode example of the scenario where we encountered the issue:
```java
List<Long> ids = new ArrayList<>(BATCH_SIZE);

// 1. Run outer query to stream ids
for (Long id : executeQuery(connection, outerSql)) {
    ids.add(id);

    if (ids.size() >= BATCH_SIZE) {
        // 2. Run inner query for the batch
        runInnerQuery(connection, ids);
        ids.clear();
    }
}

if (!ids.isEmpty()) {
    runInnerQuery(connection, ids);
}
```

The outer query returns ~2M rows, and the inner query can run 100+ times, so it's not unusual for the outer query to run for more than 20 minutes.

We observed that the outer query is marked as `FINISHED` on the Trino coordinator within a few seconds, and is eventually removed from the query history due to the query history settings (`query.max-history = 100`, `query.min-expire-age = 15m`).
At some point `client.advance()` is called in the `AsyncResultIterator`, and the Trino coordinator returns a 404 since the query is no longer in the query history, causing an exception that triggers this issue.


### Questions
For a spooled query, is it expected that the query transitions to `FINISHED` before the client has finished consuming the results from the external storage location?
I'd be happy to investigate further on my end if this is possibly a bug.

