body_text:
After #30990 a materialized view whose source is pinned with `FOR VERSION AS OF` reads the right rows, but once the source table has moved past the pinned snapshot every refresh rewrites the whole storage table even though nothing changed.

`IcebergMetadata.getTableChangeInfo` compares the recorded source snapshot with `currentSnapshot()`, so the view is reported `STALE` as soon as the source advances and the engine runs the refresh. In `IcebergSplitManager.getScan` the incremental start and end are the same pinned snapshot, the append scan cannot bound an empty interval, and the refresh falls back to delete-all plus a full re-append of the unchanged data.

Steps to reproduce (`TestIcebergMaterializedView`):

```java
try (TestTable source = newTrinoTable("pinned_source", "AS SELECT 1 AS value")) {
    long snapshotId = (long) computeScalar("SELECT snapshot_id FROM \"" + source.getName() + "$snapshots\"");
    assertUpdate("CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW pinned_mv GRACE PERIOD INTERVAL '1' DAY AS SELECT value FROM " + source.getName() + " FOR VERSION AS OF " + snapshotId);
    assertUpdate("REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW pinned_mv", 1);
    assertUpdate("INSERT INTO " + source.getName() + " VALUES 2", 1);
    assertUpdate("REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW pinned_mv", 1); // logs "Performing full MV refresh"
    assertUpdate("REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW pinned_mv", 1); // logs "Performing full MV refresh" again
}
```

`system.metadata.materialized_views` reports the view as stale from the first insert on, and each refresh adds a delete plus an append snapshot to the storage table carrying the same single row.

Expected: a refresh whose incremental interval is empty should not rewrite the storage table. Returning an empty split source while staying in incremental mode makes it a no-op append, and treating the recorded snapshot as current when it equals the snapshot the query reads would let the engine skip the refresh entirely.

