- [x] If I used AI assistance to prepare this issue, I have reviewed and followed the [AI Contribution Policy](https://typescript-eslint.io/contributing/ai-policy/).

### Playground Link

https://typescript-eslint.io/play/#ts=6.0.3&fileType=.ts&code=GYVwdgxgLglg9mABAcwKZQBTDnAXIgb0QG0AiAQ1IF18BnKAJxjGQG4TSAjaux55RAF8ANIgDWqAJ74KpRAB9EXUgEpCAKESIG6EAyTY4xCZKqt1g9UA&eslintrc=N4KABGBEBOCuA2BTAzpAXGUEKQAIBcBPABxQGNoBLY-AWhXkoDt8B6Jge1tieQEMAZonpNYAW1p9kySgHMmYxC3RRE0aB2iRwYAL4hdQA&tsconfig=N4KABGBEDGD2C2AHAlgGwKYCcDyiAuysAdgM6QBcYoEEkJemy0eFYDAruuGAL4g9A&tokens=false

### Repro Code

```TypeScript
function get(foo: { ["a"]: string; ["b"]: string }, key: "a" | "b") {
  return foo[key];
}
```

### ESLint Config

```javascript
module.exports = {
  parser: "@typescript-eslint/parser",
  rules: {
    "@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-enum-assignment": "error",
  },
};
```

### tsconfig

```jsonc
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true
  }
}
```

### Expected Result

No error.

### Actual Result

Error on `foo[key]`:
```
"The computed key used here does not have a shared enum type with the expected enum ."
```

### Additional Info

It only seems to be a problem when using bracket notation for the keys.
Something like this seems to work just fine:
```ts
function get(foo: { "a": string; "b": string }, key: "a" | "b") {
  return foo[key];
}
```

