### Suggestion

Not all multiple errors are reported if they are on the same line. For example, this is not reported:

```ts
{
  code: `
declare function cb(): number;
const foo: Array<(() => void) | false> = [false, cb, () => cb()];
  `,
  errors: [
    {
      column: 50,
      endColumn: 52,
      endLine: 3,
      line: 3,
      messageId: 'nonVoidFunc',
    },
    {
      column: 60,
      endColumn: 64,
      endLine: 3,
      line: 3,
      messageId: 'nonVoidReturn',
      suggestions: [
        {
          messageId: 'suggestAddVoidOp',
          output: `
declare function cb(): number;
const foo: Array<(() => void) | false> = [false, cb, () => void cb()];
  `,
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
},
```

However, this proves that they are different errors:
```ts
{
  code: `
declare function cb(): number;
const foo: Array<(() => void) | false> = [
  false,
  cb,
  () => cb(),
  false,
  false,
  false,
];
  `,
  errors: [
    {
      column: 50,
      endColumn: 52,
      endLine: 5,
      line: 5,
      messageId: 'nonVoidFunc',
    },
    {
      column: 60,
      endColumn: 64,
      endLine: 6,
      line: 6,
      messageId: 'nonVoidReturn',
      suggestions: [
        {
          messageId: 'suggestAddVoidOp',
          output: `
declare function cb(): number;
const foo: Array<(() => void) | false> = [
  false,
  cb,
  void () => cb(),
  false,
  false,
  false,
];
  `,
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
},
```

Thus, the rule should consider all the positions of the error.

This would be help make sure that only one error case is tested at a time.

