  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2023",
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["repro.ts"]
}
```

### Expected Result

No reported rule violations, in approximately the same time `tsc -p .` takes (~2 sec).

### Actual Result

No reported rule violations, but only after 164 sec at 545 MB peak memory usage. Almost all of that time is one TypeScript checker call made by the rule.


### Additional Info

The repro code is a reduction from a real-world case encountered using the [Claude Code mod hook-registration API](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/plugins/mods/reference#the-hook-function). In that case, the rule causes ESLint to run out of memory. A source file with one `on(...)` call fails after ~45 sec with a 2 GB heap, and after ~115 sec with an 8 GB heap. `tsc` checks that same file in under 1 sec.

**Cause.** When determining the return type a callback parameter expects, `no-misused-promises` calls `getCallSignatures()` on the declared parameter type of every overload of the callee. When the parameter type is a union, `tsutils.unionConstituents` returns the constituents of the union. However, when the parameter type is a generic type whose constraint is a union, `tsutils.unionConstituents` simply returns the generic type. When called with a generic type, `getCallSignatures()` first resolves it to its apparent type, which is its constraint. It thengets the signatures of each member of that union and merges them into union signatures, with complexity that grows polynomially to exponentially with the size of the union and its members' parameter types. `no-misused-promises` does not need the merged union signatures.

**Fix.** Use the parameter's apparent type instead (`unionConstituents(getApparentType(type))`), which resolves a generic type to its constraint, so `unionConstituents` returns the constraint's members instead of the generic type itself. The constraint's members are then checked one at a time without the merge of the union signatures. With the fix, execution cost grows linearly with the size of the union. With the change, ESLint lints this repro in under 2 sec with only this rule enabled, and just over 2 sec with the `all` preset, with no reported rule violations.

