This issue was created with the GitHub CLI, so it has no labels. Recreated as #13089 using the issue template.

> I'm a professional programmer who mainly works with TypeScript. I used Claude Code to quickly explore this repository's codebase, and I reviewed this issue myself.

### Playground Link

https://typescript-eslint.io/play/#fileType=.ts&&eslintrc=N4KABGBEBOCuA2BTAzpAXGUEKQAIBcBPABxQGNoBLY-AWhXkoDt8B6Jge1tiacTJTIAhtEK0ipWkOTJE0fJQ5N0UOdA7RI4MAF8QOoA&tsconfig=N4KABGBEDGD2C2AHAlgGwKYCcDyiAuysAdgM6QBcYoEEkJemy0eFYDAruuGAL4g9A&tokens=false

### Repro Code

```ts
type Data = { value: string };

declare function evaluate<T>(cb: () => T): T;
declare const anyValue: any;
declare const response: { json(): Promise<any> };

// reported
const a = evaluate(async () => {
  return anyValue as unknown as Data;
});
const b = evaluate(async () => {
  return response.json() as unknown as Promise<Data>;
});

// not reported (same expressions without a block body)
const c = evaluate(async () => anyValue as unknown as Data);
const d = evaluate(async () => response.json() as unknown as Promise<Data>);
```

### ESLint Config

```js
module.exports = {
  rules: {
    '@typescript-eslint/no-unnecessary-type-assertion': 'error',
  },
};
```

### tsconfig

```jsonc
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true
  }
}
```

### Expected Result

Either no report, or a fix that only drops the intermediate `as unknown`, leaving `anyValue as Data` and `response.json() as Promise<Data>`, which the rule already doesn't report.

### Actual Result

```
9:10   This assertion is unnecessary since the receiver accepts the original type of the expression.
12:10  This assertion is unnecessary since the receiver accepts the original type of the expression.
```

The autofix removes the whole double assertion, so `a` and `b` change from `Promise<Data>` to `Promise<any>`.
The same expressions in `c` and `d` are not reported.

### Additional Info

In the `return` case, the "receiver" type seems to be the function's inferred return type, which is itself derived from the assertion being checked.
`isDoubleAssertionUnnecessary` then checks `isTypeAssignableTo(originalType, contextualType)`, which is always true when the original type contains `any`.
Started in 8.59.0 (#11789).

