body_text:
This is about GPUI (not the Zed app): the X11 backend of the window visibility API added in #64107.

### Reproduction steps

On Ubuntu 24.04 with GNOME Shell 46 on X11 (a compositing window manager), at `main` b6171bc:

1. Add this example to `crates/gpui` (plus a matching `[[example]]` entry in `crates/gpui/Cargo.toml`). It draws continuously, prints the visibility and the number of frames drawn each second, minimizes itself after 3 s, and restores itself after 6 s:

<details><summary>crates/gpui/examples/visibility_minimized.rs</summary>

```rust
#[path = "example_support/fonts.rs"]
mod example_support;

use std::time::Duration;

use gpui::{App, Bounds, Context, Window, WindowBounds, WindowOptions, div, prelude::*, px, size};
use gpui_platform::application;

struct Probe {
    frames: u64,
    _visibility: gpui::Subscription,
}

impl Render for Probe {
    fn render(&mut self, window: &mut Window, _cx: &mut Context<Self>) -> impl IntoElement {
        self.frames += 1;
        window.request_animation_frame();
        div()
            .size_full()
            .bg(gpui::white())
            .child(format!("frame {}", self.frames))
    }
}

fn main() {
    application().run(|cx: &mut App| {
        if !example_support::load_fonts(cx) {
            return;
        }
        let bounds = Bounds::centered(None, size(px(300.), px(200.)), cx);
        cx.open_window(
            WindowOptions {
                window_bounds: Some(WindowBounds::Windowed(bounds)),
                ..Default::default()
            },
            |window, cx| {
                cx.new(|cx| {
                    let visibility =
                        cx.observe_window_visibility(window, |_: &mut Probe, visibility, _, _| {
                            println!("visibility changed: {visibility:?}");
                        });
                    cx.spawn_in(window, async move |this: gpui::WeakEntity<Probe>, cx| {
                        let mut last = 0;
                        for second in 1..=9 {
                            cx.background_executor().timer(Duration::from_secs(1)).await;
                            this.update_in(cx, |this, window, cx| {
                                println!(
                                    "t={second}s visibility={:?} frames_in_last_second={}",
                                    window.visibility(),
                                    this.frames - last
                                );
                                last = this.frames;
                                if second == 3 {
                                    println!("minimizing");
                                    window.minimize_window();
                                }
                                if second == 6 {
                                    println!("restoring");
                                    window.activate_window();
                                }
                                if second == 9 {
                                    cx.quit();
                                }
                            })
                            .ok();
                        }
                    })
                    .detach();
                    Probe {
                        frames: 0,
                        _visibility: visibility,
                    }
                })
            },
        )
        .unwrap();
        cx.activate(true);
    });
}
```

</details>

2. `cargo run -p gpui --example visibility_minimized` in an X11 session.
3. While the window is minimized, inspect it with `xwininfo -id <window>` and `xprop -id <window> _NET_WM_STATE WM_STATE`. Clicking the minimize button in the title bar gives the same result as `minimize_window()`.

### Current vs. Expected behavior

**Current:** the minimized window is reported as `Visible`, no visibility change is delivered, and frames are still requested:

```
visibility changed: Visible
t=1s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=62
t=2s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
t=3s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
minimizing
t=4s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=25
t=5s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=27
t=6s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=28
restoring
t=7s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
t=8s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
t=9s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
```

At that moment the X server and the window manager agree that the window is minimized, but the window stays mapped:

```
Map State: IsViewable
_NET_WM_STATE(ATOM) = _NET_WM_STATE_HIDDEN
WM_STATE(WM_STATE):
		window state: Iconic
```

GNOME Shell does not unmap minimized windows (a composited window is kept for the overview and thumbnails), and it sends neither `UnmapNotify` nor `VisibilityNotify`. The X11 backend derives visibility only from the mapped state and `VisibilityNotify` (`WindowRef::visibility` in `crates/gpui_linux/src/linux/x11/client.rs`), so under this window manager a minimized window never becomes `Hidden`. Because `update_refresh_loop` uses the same check, the periodic refresh keeps running for a window nobody can see.

**Expected:** as documented on `WindowVisibility` ("a shown window that is … minimized … is `Hidden`", and "the platform will not request frames for it until it becomes visible again"), a minimized window is `Hidden`, observers are notified, and no frames are requested until it is restored.

`X11WindowState` already tracks `_NET_WM_STATE_HIDDEN` in its `hidden` field (currently used only by `is_maximized`), so the information is there. Taking it into account and re-evaluating visibility when `_NET_WM_STATE` changes fixes it locally:

```
visibility changed: Visible
t=1s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=61
t=2s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
t=3s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
minimizing
visibility changed: Hidden
t=4s visibility=Hidden frames_in_last_second=1
t=5s visibility=Hidden frames_in_last_second=0
t=6s visibility=Hidden frames_in_last_second=0
restoring
visibility changed: Visible
t=7s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
t=8s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
t=9s visibility=Visible frames_in_last_second=60
```

<details><summary>Patch used for the run above</summary>

```diff
--- a/crates/gpui_linux/src/linux/x11/client.rs
+++ b/crates/gpui_linux/src/linux/x11/client.rs
@@ -95,7 +95,10 @@ impl WindowRef {
     /// managers rarely report `FULLY_OBSCURED`, so under them this is the
     /// mapped state alone.
     fn visibility(&self) -> WindowVisibility {
-        if self.is_mapped && !matches!(self.last_visibility, Visibility::FULLY_OBSCURED) {
+        if self.is_mapped
+            && !matches!(self.last_visibility, Visibility::FULLY_OBSCURED)
+            && !self.window.is_hidden()
+        {
             WindowVisibility::Visible
         } else {
             WindowVisibility::Hidden
@@ -986,6 +989,10 @@ impl X11Client {
                     .property_notify(event)
                     .context("X11: Failed to handle property notify")
                     .log_err();
+                let state = self.0.borrow_mut();
+                if event.atom == state.atoms._NET_WM_STATE {
+                    handle_visibility_changed(state, event.window);
+                }
             }
             Event::FocusIn(event) => {
                 let window = self.get_window(event.event)?;
--- a/crates/gpui_linux/src/linux/x11/window.rs
+++ b/crates/gpui_linux/src/linux/x11/window.rs
@@ -1345,6 +1345,11 @@ impl X11WindowStatePtr {
         }
     }

+    /// Whether the window manager marks the window `_NET_WM_STATE_HIDDEN` (minimized).
+    pub(crate) fn is_hidden(&self) -> bool {
+        self.state.borrow().hidden
+    }
+
     pub fn set_visibility(&self, visibility: WindowVisibility) {
         if std::mem::replace(&mut self.state.borrow_mut().visibility, visibility) == visibility {
             return;
```

The doc comments on `WindowRef::visibility` and on the X11 bullet of `WindowVisibility` would need a matching update ("mapped state, `_NET_WM_STATE_HIDDEN` and `VisibilityNotify`"). Only tested on GNOME Shell; the patch was not tried on other window managers.

</details>

### Zed version and system specs

Not the Zed app: GPUI from `zed-industries/zed` `main` at b6171bc (2026-09-17), `gpui_linux` X11 backend.
OS: Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS, Linux 6.8.0, GNOME Shell 46.0, X11 session (Composite enabled)
