### Summary

On Linux and FreeBSD, `Editor::selections_did_change` writes to the primary selection whenever there is more than one cursor, even when every cursor is empty, and it resolves every selection to do so on each keystroke.

### Description

`crates/editor/src/selection.rs` (`selections_did_change`, the block under `// Copy selections to primary selection buffer`, `main` at b6171bcc98):

```rust
#[cfg(any(target_os = "linux", target_os = "freebsd"))]
if local {
    let selections = self
        .selections
        .all::<MultiBufferOffset>(&self.display_snapshot(cx));
    let buffer_handle = self.buffer.read(cx).read(cx);

    let mut text = String::new();
    for (index, selection) in selections.iter().enumerate() {
        let text_for_selection = buffer_handle
            .text_for_range(selection.start..selection.end)
            .collect::<String>();

        text.push_str(&text_for_selection);
        if index != selections.len() - 1 {
            text.push('\n');
        }
    }

    if !text.is_empty() {
        cx.write_to_primary(ClipboardItem::new_string(text));
    }
}
```

With N empty cursors the joined text is N-1 newlines, so `!text.is_empty()` is true and the primary selection is overwritten with blank lines on every selection change: every keystroke while typing with multiple cursors, and every cursor movement. With a single empty cursor the text is empty and nothing is written, which looks like the intended behaviour.

Steps that should show it (I have not run this on Linux; the report is from reading the code, so please treat the steps as expected rather than verified):

1. Select some text in another application so the primary selection has content.
2. In Zed, add a second cursor (for example `cmd-alt-down`/`ctrl-alt-down`) and type a character, or just move the cursors.
3. Middle-click paste in the other application. Expected: the original selection. Actual (by this reading): a newline.

Separately, the block resolves all selections through `all::<MultiBufferOffset>` on every selection change, which with thousands of cursors is a per-keystroke O(N) cost that the non-Linux path does not pay (see #32051). Skipping the write when all selections are empty would fix both.

### Zed Version and System Specs

Found while profiling #32051 on `main` at b6171bcc98. Linux-only code path; observed by reading, not reproduced.

